Z||000005||Fierowrecker||07-21-2001||06:07 AM||||Hey Craig!<BR>Don't the new Buicks have a super charger?<BR>Might try some of the bone yards!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||07-21-2001||11:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B><BR>Don't the new Buicks have a super charger?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would be a trick. The roots type blowers on the 3800 would be great if their were a manifold to bolt it to. The 3800 is 90 degree block, allowing more room for the sc to sit down in the V of the engine. The 2.8 is a 60 degree. Not so easy. Even if you were able to fabricate a new manifold, you would likely have to hack out huge portions of the decklid for clearance.<P>My 2¢||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_24||07-21-2001||01:00 PM||||I may have to go turbo.  I just figured I would try to find out about superchargers first.  I am building a 2.8 with 0 miles with every performance part money can buy (within reason).  I am going to add roller rockers, headers, performance cam, performance crank, and possibly a 6 fogger Nitrous wet kit since I am building the motor I can maake it handle the Nitrous.  I just figured a nice supercharger for a cheap price might also be nice.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||7
Z||000008||Mach10||07-21-2001||01:08 PM||||I don't believe that turbos are all that great when compared to a similar quality blower:<BR>1) The blower makes boost at all RPMs, which smooths the powerband, and raises performance across the board.<BR>2) The turbo running off the exhaust would leach just as much power as a supercharger. Conservation Of Energy. It costs just X Joules of energy to force N PSI of boost into the engine. <BR>3) Superchargers sound RAD.<BR>4) Due to the construction, Turbos have potential for greater HP gains, however almost NO-ONE is going to want to run 60psi into their motor. With a supercharger, you can get 60psi of boost, but that will kill out your motor at idle.<BR>5) Superchargers run cooler, reducing the need for a separate intercooler (though one still helps). Turbos run the risk of detonation at lower PSIs for this reason. Having said that, properly tuned, both can run happily forever. Poorly tuned, and you can litter the road equally with bent parts. <P>Back on topic: I may know of someone in this city who has a blower that'll fit in the 2.8 I'm going to go talk to him on Monday.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||MinnGreenGT||07-21-2001||02:00 PM||||I'm also a member of the GM 60degree v6 performance forum... there is a post there about a new company releasing a superchager at the end of the summer, but it is built for the GM 3100 (3.1L) engine- probably adaptable... I'll repost if I can find the exact website.  Try to search on Google for 3.1 performance...||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||07-21-2001||02:11 PM||||Try this thread: <A HREF="http://www.gmforums.com," TARGET=_blank>www.gmforums.com,</A>  click on "forums homepage" - Then under "club corner" click on "60° V6 Pushrod Performance" it should be listed under the "performance" section, heading "Supercharger for 3.1 "<P><A HREF="http://discussions.gmforums.com/showthread.php?s=58ec9f23389baf955a118cf1f96a8d2d&threadid=5607" TARGET=_blank> <A HREF="http://discussions.gmforums.com/showthread.php?s=58ec9f23389baf955a118cf1f96a8d2d&threadid=5607</A>" TARGET=_blank>http://discussions.gmforums.com/showthread.php?s=58ec9f23389baf955a118cf1f96a8d2d&threadid=5607[/url]</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiero_24||07-21-2001||04:59 PM||||Darn, $3500 is a good penny to spend.  Since I am building the motor, I could always make it a 3.1 instead (plus a supercharger).  Mach10, I could not agree more on the Turbo issue, thats why I want a supercharger.  Since I have a 3 speed auto I do not have much high end speed, so the turbo lag will hold me back on the line.  With a supercharger you always have the power there.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||12
Z||000012||Rogue_Ant||07-21-2001||05:12 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR>I don't believe that turbos are all that great when compared to a similar quality blower:<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hmm, ok opinions are welcome, but lets make sure everyone understands the difference between a Centrifigul Supercharger and a Blower.  Yes they are both considered Superchargers since they are both belt driven.  <BR>A Centrifigul supercharger produces boost depending on RPM (comparable to a Turbo) and usually sits off to the side of the engine like an alternator would.<P>A Roots type supercharger (blower)(think 3.8 supercharger) sits on top of the intake manifold and will produce full boost at a relatively low RPM.<BR>But because it produces a lot of boost in the lower RPM band, you will need to modify your fuel system so it doesn't detonate your engine (that is if you plan on running very much boost, i.e. &gt;6psi)<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>1) The blower makes boost at all RPMs, which smooths the power band, and raises performance across the board.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yep, just talked about that.<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>2) The turbo running off the exhaust would leach just as much power as a supercharger. Conservation Of Energy. It costs just X Joules of energy to force N PSI of boost into the engine.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Um wrong.  A turbo uses 'free' energy to make boost.  A turbo utilizes energy that is otherwise being wasted, where as a Supercharger pulls off of the crank like a normal accessory (think air conditioner).  Also a supercharger makes the crank wear prematurely (although this may not be a factor if your car last 100,000 miles or 120,000).<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>3) Superchargers sound RAD.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Lol, yes they do.  But that whine can get very annoying on long trips, and not to mention that it is a give-away that you got a fast car.<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>4) Due to the construction, Turbos have potential for greater HP gains, however almost NO-ONE is going to want to run 60psi into their motor. With a supercharger, you can get 60psi of boost, but that will kill out your motor at idle.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Ok, now that is true, especially if you have a roots type supercharger.<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>5) Superchargers run cooler, reducing the need for a separate intercooler (though one still helps). Turbos run the risk of detonation at lower PSIs for this reason.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yes this is one disadvantage of using the 'free' energy from the exhaust:  the exhaust is very hot and thus heats up the turbo.  Because the turbo is hot it also heats up the intake charge.  The use of intercooler is a very effective way of combating this problem.<P>Hmm, ok lets just look at pros and cons real quick.<P>Pros for Supercharger:<BR>Instantanious power<BR>Intercooler not necessary<BR>Sounds Cool<P>Cons for SuperCharger:<BR>Expensive $$$<BR>Hard to find for 60degree engine<BR>Abnormal crank wear<BR>Won't make as much power as a turbo<P>OK now<P>Pros for a Turbo:<BR>Doesn't take power to make power<BR>Much more common, can find used ones for cheap (just look on Ebay)<BR>Don't have to modify fuel system as much<BR>Doesn't make as much noise.<P>Cons:<BR>Dramatically heats up the intake charge, thus requiring a intercooler<BR>Intercooler costs extra $$$<BR>Turbo Lag<P>Ok now this is getting long, but let me just throw out one more fact.<BR>In no professional race in the last 20 years that allows forced induction has a supercharge been effective, all of the leaders have been _TurboCharged_.<P><BR>Rogue<p>[This message has been edited by Rogue_Ant (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||07-21-2001||06:12 PM||||I always understood that the backpressure caused by actually moving the impeller causes exactly the same HP "loss." as you would off the crank. After the exhaust has burned, no more energy is caused by it. The engine has to work to get it out. Any restriction in getting it out results in a loss, ostensibly even the turbo. Now, the Turbo WOULD be a more efficient device, as it has less moving parts, and there's no inherent friction from a belt driven system... but I still prefer superchargers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually, in either system, there can be no "loss" as the net is ALWAYS gain. 140HP - 3hp + 50hp etc... I firmly believe that there is no free lunch (except for nitrous  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>And yes, I didn't differentiate the Roots and the centrifical blower... Sorry. ||||reg||12
Z||000014||Rogue_Ant||07-21-2001||07:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>And yes, I didn't differentiate the Roots and the centrifical blower... Sorry. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Lol, yes sorry it wasn't a flame, I was just trying to explain the difference because not everyone is fluent in the different types of forced inductions.<P>Rogue<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||07-21-2001||08:53 PM||||I think we'll get along Jeeeeesssst Fine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Welcome to the forum!||||reg||6
Z||000016||Rogue_Ant||07-21-2001||09:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>I think we'll get along Jeeeeesssst Fine!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Welcome to the forum!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks, glad there is support out there.  I live in Colorado Springs, and the big thing is imports, think I've seen 4 total fieros in the whole city (around 100,000 people)<P>Rogue<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||07-21-2001||09:44 PM||||The majority of the heat from either a turbo or a supercharger comes from compressing air. A supercharger needs an intercooler just as much as a turbo.<P>There is energy in the exhaust in the form of heat exhaust gas temps downstream of a turbo drop dramatically. That's why it takes a long time to get a cat to light off when it has a turbo ahead of it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||hugh||07-22-2001||11:44 AM||||I have a GN,so naturally I prefer turbochargers.One thing that has not been mentioned which is extremely important;With a supercharger,cam selection is not critical.You can run anything from a stock to radical cam.With a turbocharger if you run a cam with too much overlap you will drop exhaust temperature and kill any gains you may have gotten with the turbo,in other words;it won't work.You would be better off with a stock cam with a turbo than an aftermarket. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Will||07-22-2001||03:15 PM||||Cam slection is also critical for a supercharger, just in a different way than a turbocharger.<P>Most turbocharged engines run more back pressure than boost, so if the cam has too much overlap reversion is a very real possiblity.<P>In a supercharged engine, intake pressure is much higher than exhaust pressure, so if the cam has too much overlap the engine may have excessive blowby and lower effective compression, not to mention wasting boost and energy.<P>A supercharger cam isn't as application specific or as different from an NA cam as a turbocharger cam is, but it is still needs to be considered.<BR>The ideal cam for an engine will also change slightly with different boost pressures<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 07-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||BBBAD GT||07-22-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rogue_Ant:<BR><B>   [QUOTE]<BR>In no professional race in the last 20 years that allows forced induction has a supercharge been effective, all of the leaders have been _TurboCharged_.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think NHRA qualifies as a 'professional race', and there isn't one turbo in Top Fuel or Funny Car........<P>Just pointing that out, no flame (I own both a turbo Stang and a centrifugal supercharged Stang, fun stuff).<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000021||TRiAD||07-23-2001||10:44 AM||||Rogue, some good info, some not so good. <P>Centrifugal superchargers do generally sit on the side, and do produce boost in a non-linear fashion...more boost with more RPMs, and are really not very proper for automotive use. Don't get me wrong, there are a lot of kits for them out there, but in order for them to make sufficient boost off idle, they make WAY too much in the top end. Every system I've seen that uses them includes a blow-off valve for that reason. They allow excess pressure to escape at shifts and throttle-lift, but also allow the excess to escape at higher RPMs. <P>Roots-type superchargers generally sit on top (or behind), but can be placed anywhere. They produce linear boost, and can produce full boost at very low RPMs. They are not efficient, and create a LOT of heat, but are a complete BALL! These are the kits used by most every auto manufacturer who offers SC on their cars, and are the ones used in professional motorsports.<P>Turbochargers can be mounted anywhere, but as with ALL pressurization techniques, the shorter the piping the better. Turbos do NOT produce "free" HP, nothing does. Turbos produce significant losses at low RPMs due to the blockage to the exhaust system, and the fact that they must be spun by that exhaust before they can produce any power. Don't be fooled, "turbo-lag" is not just the time before the power comes on, it's a LOSS of power caused by spinning the impeller with the exhaust. <P>Superchargers in general are more reliable than Turbos, create boost at all RPMs, and require less maintenance. Turbos require lubrication in conjunction with the engine, require warm-up and cool-down periods with EVERY use, and will destroy themselves very quickly if allowed to spin without the oil pump running (you must let them spin down with the engine at idle before you can turn off the engine. This is what "turbo-timers" are. They allow you to remove your key and lock the car, and it will idle for a pre-determined period of time, then shut-off.). <P>As you can tell, I prefer Superchargers, but I have seen how Turbos have advanced in the last 10 years, and they are getting better...there are just many limitations inherent in the design. <P>Oh, and all turbos and superchargers can be called "blowers", it's not limited to Roots-type superchargers. <P>And in Europe (help me out here if you're from there...) I believe Turbos are called "exhaust-driven superchargers". <P>Just through I'd clear the air a little. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula||07-23-2001||10:55 AM||||I think Carrol Shelby said that.....<BR>if it has turbo lag, your not driving it right.<BR>....from the 80s shelby dodge cars.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigformulagt.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||07-23-2001||12:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B> I think NHRA qualifies as a 'professional race', and there isn't one turbo in Top Fuel or Funny Car........<P>Just pointing that out, no flame (I own both a turbo Stang and a centrifugal supercharged Stang, fun stuff).<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought the rules prohibited turbos in Top Fuel and Funny Car...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||BBBAD GT||07-23-2001||01:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B><BR>Centrifugal superchargers do generally sit on the side, and do produce boost in a non-linear fashion...more boost with more RPMs, and are really not very proper for automotive use. Don't get me wrong, there are a lot of kits for them out there, but in order for them to make sufficient boost off idle, they make WAY too much in the top end. Every system I've seen that uses them includes a blow-off valve for that reason. They allow excess pressure to escape at shifts and throttle-lift, but also allow the excess to escape at higher RPMs. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think you mean the centrifugal blower is linear in boost to RPMs, and the positive displacement blower isn't.  Also, I'm not sure what you mean above, the blow-off valve you are talking about is simply a bypass for shifts/major deceleration (to relieve the pressure between the blower and throttle body), and are not designed to bleed off boost at high RPMs at WOT.  I'm talking the basic Vortech/Paxton/Powerdyne centrifugal blowers.  The centrifugal blowers are very efficent at high RPMs, and want every last pound of boost it can produce.  <P>The positive displacement blowers are excellent for the street (just like Triad pointed out), but aren't big enough to flow the air centrifugal blower do at high RPMs (at least the ones avail I've seen).    <P>Tuning is completely different with each individual type of forced induction.<BR><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Wannabe||07-23-2001||03:12 PM||||Around 15 years ago, BMW's 4 cyl, 1.5L turbo F1 qualifying engine was putting out around 1250 hp. At the time, V6 turbos for F1 racing had been under development by various teams for about 8 years. Imagine the power potential if that development had been allowed to continue until today. The factory turbo cars that you can buy today exhibit almost none of the drawbacks that the factory turbos of the eighties did. Turbos have come a long, long way.||||reg||1
Z||000026||artherd||07-23-2001||07:12 PM||||Heck, flat-12 boxer engines of similar displacement (1500cc) were making in the range of 1,500bhp on a daily basis. I belive these were the competition engines as well.<P>The turbo cars were just too fast :)<BR>(too dangerous, 240 is SCARY any way you slice! :P)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000027||Johnny07||07-23-2001||08:56 PM||||If you want to get real detailed on your turbo vs. supercharger decisions get informed by checking out a book called "Automotive Supercharging and Turbocharging Systems" by John D. Humphries. The ISBN # is : 0879386576  My searches state that it is out of print but I am sure they printed quite a few.<BR> All of the information discussed in this post is covered plus the technical engineering backup proof for each example. It also covers exhaust,induction,cooling,engine control systems(electronics)and even emissions.<P>If you want to see what type of system might be the future check out the Greenpeace SmILE Car.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.greenpeace.org/~climate/smile/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.greenpeace.org/~climate/smile/</A> <P>It's not a fast car but it's 2 cylinder econobox with the engine sized to maximize low gas consumption. Of course realize that if your goal was instead to say maximize acceleration and power instead...well enough said about that you can figure out what to do instead<P><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Rogue_Ant||07-23-2001||10:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B> I think NHRA qualifies as a 'professional race', and there isn't one turbo in Top Fuel or Funny Car........<P>Just pointing that out, no flame (I own both a turbo Stang and a centrifugal supercharged Stang, fun stuff).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lol, true, but that is only for a 1/4 mile, most racing (and street driving) is a lot longer then a 1/4 mile.<P>Rogue<BR>||||reg||1
